// In-depth battles, batch b: Plateia, Leuctra, Maling, Queroneia, Isso, Hidaspes.

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "hoplita": "A heavy infantry soldier of the Greek cities, with a big round shield (the hoplon), spear and bronze helmet. He fought shoulder to shoulder with his neighbors, each shield also protecting the man beside him.",
  "hilotas": "The serf population of Sparta, tied to the land and forced to work for the citizens. They were far more numerous than the Spartans, who lived in fear of a revolt.",
  "esparciatas": "The full citizens of Sparta, trained from the age of 7 for war alone. They were few, a few thousand, and every one who died was missed.",
  "batalhão sagrado": "An elite Theban force of 300 soldiers, organized, according to tradition, in 150 pairs of lovers, so that no one would flee in front of his companion.",
  "ala recusada": "The part of the line that the general deliberately keeps held back and far from the enemy, while the other wing attacks. It does not need to win, it just must not be destroyed.",
  "peltastas": "Greek light infantry, with a small shield and javelins. Fast, it was used for skirmishes, ambushes and harassing the heavy infantry.",
  "besta": "A bow mounted on a wooden stock, with a trigger. It takes little strength to shoot and little training to aim. The Chinese used it in mass centuries before Europe.",
  "reinos combatentes": "A period of Chinese history (about 475 to 221 BC) in which seven great states fought one another until Qin unified the country.",
  "liga de corinto": "An alliance of Greek cities created by Philip II in 337 BC, with himself as military leader. In practice, it was Macedonian domination dressed up as an agreement.",
  "mercenários": "Soldiers who fight for pay, not for their own city or king. The Persian kings hired many Greek hoplites.",
  "cárdaces": "Young Persian infantry, trained in the manner of Greek hoplites. Little is known for certain about how they fought.",
  "elefantes de guerra": "Elephants trained for battle, led by a mahout. They frightened men and horses, but, wounded or in panic, they could trample their own army.",
  "martelo e bigorna": "A tactic in which the infantry holds the enemy from the front (the anvil) while the cavalry attacks from behind or from the flank (the hammer)."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  plateia: {
    war: "Second Persian invasion of Greece (480–479 BC)",
    hook: "Eleven days of frayed nerves, a retreat that went wrong and the end of the Persian invasions.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 480 BC, the Persian king Xerxes crossed the Dardanelles strait with the largest army the Greeks had ever seen. He rolled over Leonidas' 300 at Thermopylae and occupied and burned Athens. But, in September, his fleet was shattered in the bay of Salamis, and Xerxes went back to Asia.",
        "He left behind his best general, Mardonius, with the chosen part of the army, to finish the job the following year. Mardonius spent the winter in Thessaly and tried to divide the Greeks: he offered Athens peace and the rebuilding of the city if it switched sides. Athens refused. Mardonius came back and occupied Athens for the second time.",
        { h: "The road to Plataea" },
        { timeline: [
          ["August 480 BC", "Thermopylae: the Persians win and open up central Greece."],
          ["September 480 BC", "Salamis: the Greek fleet defeats the Persian one. Xerxes returns to Asia."],
          ["Winter 480/479", "Mardonius stays in Thessaly and tries to draw Athens to the Persian side."],
          ["June 479 BC", "Mardonius occupies Athens again. Sparta at last sends its army."],
          ["August 479 BC", "The two armies meet near Plataea, in Boeotia."]
        ]},
        "Sparta was slow to march out, busy with a religious festival and with the wall on the Isthmus of Corinth. When it did, it came with everything: the largest Greek army assembled until then, under the command of Pausanias. Mardonius withdrew to Boeotia, flat ground, good for his cavalry, and a region of allies, because Thebes was on the Persian side."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Pausanias of Sparta" },
        "Regent of Sparta, because the king, Leonidas' son, was a child. He was young and commanded the entire Greek coalition. At Plataea he showed more patience than brilliance: he waited, withdrew when he had to and did not attack until the sacrifices to the gods gave a favorable sign. Years later he was accused of negotiating with the Persians and died of hunger, walled up in a temple where he had sought asylum.",
        { h: "Mardonius" },
        "Nephew and brother-in-law of Xerxes, he had been one of the greatest champions of the invasion. According to Herodotus, he knew that going back defeated would be worse than dying. His strategy was to use the cavalry to wear down the Greeks and wait for the alliance, full of rival cities, to fall apart on its own.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Aristides commanded the Athenians. On the Persian side, Masistius led the cavalry and Artabazus commanded a large contingent of his own, which Herodotus counts at 40,000 men and which played a curious part at the end."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Herodotus gives the Greeks about 110,000 men, counting light troops and [[helots|hilotas]]. Sparta's force alone is said to have had 5,000 [[Spartiates|esparciatas]], 5,000 perioikoi and 35,000 helots. Modern historians accept that it was a large army, perhaps 40,000 [[hoplites|hoplita]] and many light troops.",
        "For the Persians, Herodotus speaks of 300,000 men plus 50,000 allied Greeks, and Diodorus of 500,000. These are numbers from the ancient sources and certainly exaggerated. Modern estimates are between 70,000 and 120,000, with a strong cavalry.",
        { note: "The numbers for Plataea come almost entirely from Herodotus, who wrote a generation later. Historians agree that the Persians were more numerous, but not by how much." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The Persians camped north of the Asopus river, in a fortified camp. The Greeks first stayed on the slopes of Mount Cithaeron, where the cavalry could not reach. Between the two lay an open plain.",
        { h: "Days of waiting" },
        "Nobody wanted to attack. The seers on both sides said the sacrifices were favorable only to whoever defended. Mardonius sent the cavalry to provoke. It attacked the Megarians, who were at the most exposed point, and almost broke them, until a group of Athenians with archers came to help. Masistius fell from his horse, hit by an arrow, and was killed. The Persians, in mourning, shaved their heads and the manes of their horses.",
        "Encouraged, Pausanias moved down closer to the river, where the Gargaphia spring was. It was a mistake. On the plain, the Persian cavalry began to attack every day, intercepted the food convoys coming over Cithaeron and ended up clogging the spring, the only water for the Greek army.",
        { h: "The night retreat" },
        { formation: true },
        "Without water or food, the Greeks decided to retreat at night to a place near Plataea that Herodotus calls 'the island', between two arms of a stream. The retreat went entirely wrong. The Greek center walked too far and ended up at the temple of Hera, near the city. A Spartan officer, Amompharetus, refused to retreat in front of the enemy, according to Herodotus, and Pausanias lost hours trying to persuade him. At dawn, the Spartans and the Tegeans were alone on a hillside, and the Athenians far away, at another point.",
        "Mardonius saw the empty Greek camp and thought it was a flight. He crossed the Asopus and ordered an attack.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "The Persians stopped near the Spartans, made a wall with their wicker shields and poured down arrows. Pausanias, according to Herodotus, waited for the sacrifices to give a favorable sign, while his men fell without being able to answer. The Tegeans could not stand it and advanced first. The Spartans went right after them.",
        "The fight at the shield wall was hard. Herodotus says the Persians were no less brave, but they had light armor and fought in small groups against a line of bronze [[hoplites|hoplita]]. Mardonius fought on horseback, with a thousand chosen men, and where he was the Persians held. Until a Spartan named Arimnestus killed him with a stone. With the chief dead, the Persian center fled to the camp.",
        "At the other end, the Athenians faced the Greeks allied to the Persians, above all the Thebans, who fought hard before pulling back. Artabazus, who did not trust Mardonius, did not even join the fight: when he saw the rout, he took his men away to the north and returned to Asia.",
        "The Greeks surrounded the fortified camp. The Spartans did not know how to attack walls. The Athenians did and opened the breach, with the Tegeans entering first. What came after was a massacre."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Herodotus says only 3,000 Persians from the camp survived, besides Artabazus' men, and that the Greeks lost 159. Modern estimates for the Persians are between 50,000 and 90,000 dead. For the Greeks, Plutarch speaks of 1,360 and Diodorus of more than 10,000.",
        "The plunder was enormous. With part of it the Greeks made an offering at Delphi: a golden tripod on a bronze column in the shape of three intertwined serpents, with the names of the cities that fought.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Plataea ended the Persian invasions of Greece. Never again did a Persian army set foot on mainland Greece. The Greeks went on the offensive, and Athens, which led the war at sea from then on, entered its golden age: the Parthenon, tragedy, democracy at its height.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "On the same day, according to Herodotus, the Greek fleet defeated the Persians at Mycale, on the coast of Asia. The coincidence is so perfect that some historians are suspicious." },
        { note: "The Serpent Column still exists. The emperor Constantine took it to Constantinople, and it stands to this day in the old hippodrome of Istanbul, without the heads." },
        { note: "Amompharetus: Herodotus tells of his stubbornness as insubordination. Some historians think he was actually carrying out an order to cover the rear." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  leuctra: {
    war: "Boeotian War (378–371 BC)",
    hook: "A summer afternoon on which Sparta, unbeaten for centuries, lost in an hour what had taken generations to build.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After defeating Athens in the Peloponnesian War, in 404 BC, Sparta was the master of Greece. The other Greeks feared and detested it. In 386 BC, a treaty sponsored by the Persian king, the King's Peace, guaranteed the 'autonomy' of all Greek cities. Sparta used that clause to dismantle any alliance that could rival it, starting with the Boeotian League, led by Thebes.",
        "In 382 BC, a Spartan force seized the citadel of Thebes in the middle of peacetime. Three years later, Theban exiles returned in disguise, killed the rulers friendly to Sparta and freed the city. The Boeotian war began.",
        { h: "The road to Leuctra" },
        { timeline: [
          ["382 BC", "Sparta occupies the citadel of Thebes, the Cadmea."],
          ["379 BC", "Theban exiles, among them Pelopidas, free the city."],
          ["378–375 BC", "War in Boeotia. Thebes rebuilds the Boeotian League."],
          ["June 371 BC", "Peace conference in Sparta. Thebes wants to sign for all of Boeotia; Sparta refuses and excludes it."],
          ["July 371 BC", "King Cleombrotus invades Boeotia and meets the Thebans at Leuctra."]
        ]},
        "At the conference in 371 BC, Epaminondas, who represented Thebes, insisted on signing in the name of all the Boeotians. The Spartan king Agesilaus had Thebes struck from the treaty. The Thebans were alone. Twenty days later, the Spartan army, which was in Phocis, entered Boeotia by a mountain road south of Mount Helicon and camped on the plain of Leuctra, some 15 km from Thebes."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Epaminondas" },
        "A Theban from a noble but poor family, he had studied philosophy with a Pythagorean and was known as incorruptible and a man of few words. At Leuctra he was one of the seven Boeotarchs, the generals of the League, and he persuaded his colleagues to fight when some wanted to shut themselves inside the city. His reasoning was simple: if Sparta's head fell, the rest would fall with it.",
        { h: "Pelopidas" },
        "A close friend of Epaminondas and a hero of the liberation of Thebes. He commanded the [[Sacred Band|batalhão sagrado]], the elite 300.",
        { h: "Cleombrotus I" },
        "One of the two kings of Sparta. He was known as unaggressive toward Thebes, and his enemies at home said he sympathized with the Thebans. According to Xenophon, he was warned that, if he did not fight this time, he would be put on trial on his return. In other words: he was obliged to attack."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The most accepted modern estimate is about 10,000 [[hoplites|hoplita]] and 1,000 horsemen on the Spartan side, with only 700 [[Spartiates|esparciatas]], the full citizens. The Thebans and Boeotians would have had some 6,000 to 7,000 hoplites and about 700 horsemen. The ancient sources vary widely: Plutarch gives low numbers, and late authors such as Polyaenus reach 40,000 Spartans, which nobody takes seriously.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A small plain between hills, with the two camps face to face. The field was narrow enough that the cavalry fought in front of the infantries.",
        { h: "Epaminondas' idea" },
        "In those days, hoplite battles had almost rules of etiquette. The best troop stood on the right wing, the place of honor. The two lines advanced 8 to 12 men deep, the right of each side beat the left of the other, and whoever won first won. Sparta, with the king and the elite on the right, almost always won first.",
        "Epaminondas turned everything upside down. He put the Thebans on the left wing, facing the king and the Spartiates, in a column at least 50 ranks deep, according to Xenophon. The rest of the Boeotians stood thinner, on the right and held back on a diagonal: this was the [[refused wing|ala recusada]], with orders not to let itself be enveloped.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "The battle, move by move" },
        "First, an incident. The baggage carriers and servants of the Boeotian army wanted to leave before the fight. [[Peltasts|peltastas]] and horsemen on the Spartan side attacked them, and they ran back to the Theban army, which, according to Xenophon, became even more numerous.",
        "Second, the cavalry. The two cavalries clashed between the lines. The Theban one, trained in years of war, was far better. The Spartan one fled and crashed into its own infantry, which was still forming up.",
        "Third, the Spartans tried to adjust. According to Plutarch, Cleombrotus began to extend his right wing to envelop the Theban column. It was in that moment of disorder that Pelopidas launched the Sacred Band at a run, before the maneuver was finished.",
        "Fourth, the column struck. Instead of fighting line against line, it was like a 50-rank battering ram against a 12-rank wall. The fighting around the king was fierce: Cleombrotus was mortally wounded, and the Spartans fought to get his body out of the melee, which they managed.",
        "Fifth, the elite gave way. The Spartiates, with the king fallen and many officers dead, withdrew to the camp. The allies of the Spartan left wing, who had barely fought, withdrew with them. The Boeotian refused wing did not even need to enter real combat.",
        "In the camp, some Spartans wanted to go back and recover the dead by force. The older men saw it was not possible. They sent to ask for a truce to collect the bodies, which, in Greece, was admitting defeat."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Xenophon counts almost 1,000 Lacedaemonians dead, of whom about 400 of the 700 Spartiates present. Diodorus gives more than 4,000. On the Boeotian side, Diodorus speaks of some 300 dead, and Pausanias of only 47.",
        "For Sparta, the 400 Spartiates were a catastrophe. The city had fewer and fewer full citizens and did not know how to replace them. Aristotle would say later that Sparta could not withstand a single blow and fell for lack of men.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The following winter, Epaminondas invaded Laconia itself, something nobody had done for centuries, and freed Messenia, the region whose [[helots|hilotas]] fed Sparta. Without them, Sparta was never again a great power. Thebes dominated Greece for a decade, until the death of Epaminondas at Mantinea, in 362 BC.",
        "Among the hostages living in Thebes in those years was a teenage Macedonian prince, Philip. Historians think he learned there the lesson of concentrating force at one point and refusing the rest. His son Alexander would use it all the way to India.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The 'oblique order': the name is modern. Diodorus and Plutarch describe the diagonal line; Xenophon, who was a friend of Sparta and the only contemporary, barely mentions the Theban tactic and explains the defeat by the cavalry and bad luck." },
        { note: "Where was the Sacred Band? No source says clearly. Most historians place it at the front of the column; a 19th-century theory, that Pelopidas attacked the flank from behind the column, is now rejected by most." },
        { note: "Innovation or not? The historian Victor Davis Hanson thinks deep columns already existed in Boeotia and that the novelty was aiming them straight at the Spartan elite." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  maling: {
    war: "Wars between Wei and Qi, Warring States period (4th century BC)",
    hook: "A revenge years in the making, campfires that shrank each night and a sentence written on a tree.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 4th century BC, China was not one country but a board of rival states, in the period historians call the [[Warring States|reinos combatentes]]. One of the strongest was Wei, in the middle of the Yellow River plain. Its neighbors lived by alliances that changed with every war.",
        "Almost everything we know about Maling comes from a single source: the 'Records of the Grand Historian' by Sima Qian, written some 250 years later, which tells the story as a revenge between two men.",
        { h: "The road to Maling" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Before 354 BC", "Pang Juan, general of Wei, has Sun Bin mutilated; he flees to Qi."],
          ["354–353 BC", "Wei besieges Handan, capital of Zhao. Qi attacks Wei instead of rescuing Zhao, and wins at Guiling."],
          ["About 342 BC", "Wei attacks Han, which asks Qi for help."],
          ["342 or 341 BC", "Qi attacks the territory of Wei. Pang Juan rushes back and falls into the ambush at Maling."]
        ]},
        "According to Sima Qian, Sun Bin and Pang Juan studied the art of war together. Pang Juan became a general of Wei, but knew his colleague was better. He summoned him to Wei, accused him of a crime and had his feet (or kneecaps, depending on the translation) cut off and his face tattooed, so that he could never appear in public. Sun Bin feigned madness and escaped hidden in the carriage of an envoy from Qi."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Sun Bin" },
        "According to tradition, a descendant of Sun Tzu, the author of 'The Art of War'. Unable to walk, he could not be a general. In Qi, he became an adviser to the general Tian Ji and accompanied him to war in a covered cart. He first became famous, according to the story, for teaching Tian Ji to win horse races against the king: to bet his worst horse against the opponent's best, and lose one to win two.",
        { h: "Pang Juan" },
        "A general of Wei, veteran and proud. He had already been tricked by Sun Bin once, at Guiling. At Maling, he wanted above all a quick and complete victory.",
        { h: "Tian Ji" },
        "A general of Qi, officially the commander of the army. His great quality, in this story, was listening to Sun Bin."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The sources speak of armies of about 100,000 men on each side. These are numbers from the chronicles and probably exaggerated; there is no way to check them. Wei mobilized its entire army, under the nominal command of Crown Prince Shen, with Pang Juan as general.",
        { h: "The bait" },
        "Wei was attacking Han. Sun Bin advised waiting until the two had worn each other down and only then acting. When Qi acted, it repeated the trick of Guiling: instead of going to rescue Han, it marched straight into Wei territory, toward the capital, Daliang. Pang Juan dropped the siege and went back to defend home.",
        { h: "The shrinking campfires" },
        "Sun Bin knew that the soldiers of Wei despised those of Qi as cowards. He decided to use that. He ordered the army to retreat and, each night, to light fewer cooking fires in the camp: on the first day, fires for 100,000 men; on the second, for 50,000; on the third, for 20,000, according to Sima Qian.",
        "Pang Juan counted the fires and was delighted. According to the account, he said he had known the soldiers of Qi were cowards, and that in three days more than half had deserted. He left behind his heavy infantry and the baggage and set off after Qi with only a light, fast force, marching day and night.",
        { h: "The defile" },
        "Sun Bin calculated the speed of the pursuit and concluded that Pang Juan would reach Maling at nightfall. Maling was a narrow pass, with slopes full of obstacles, excellent for an ambush. There he hid 10,000 crossbowmen on both sides of the road, with orders to shoot all together when they saw a light.",
        "Then he had the bark of a large tree scraped off and written on the trunk: 'Pang Juan will die under this tree.'",
        { h: "The night" },
        "Pang Juan arrived at night, as predicted. He saw something written on the tree and ordered a torch lit to read it. Before he finished, the [[crossbows|besta]] fired from all sides. The army of Wei, tired, in the dark and squeezed into the defile, panicked and fell apart.",
        "According to Sima Qian, Pang Juan saw that all was lost and cut his own throat, saying he had made that 'brat' famous. The army of Qi took advantage of the victory, destroyed the rest of the troops of Wei and captured Prince Shen."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Wei lost its army, its general and its heir. It never regained its earlier strength. The balance of the [[Warring States|reinos combatentes]] shifted, and in the following century it was the kingdom of Qin, in the west, that grew on the ruins of Wei and ended up unifying China in 221 BC.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Maling is one of the most cited examples of war by cunning: deceiving the enemy about one's own strength, exploiting his pride and choosing the place and time of the fight. It is the 'Art of War' in practice, and it became a Chinese expression: 'reducing the stoves' (jian zao) means pretending weakness to lure the enemy.",
        "It is also one of the first battles in which the [[crossbow|besta]] appears deciding a combat en masse, centuries before the weapon spread through Europe.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "How much of this happened? The story is too beautiful not to have been polished: the revenge, the tree, the torch. Historians accept the campaign and the defeat of Wei; the dramatic details come from Sima Qian and should be read as tradition." },
        { note: "The date and the place: the battle is dated to 342 or 341 BC, depending on the chronology used. The exact location of Maling is also debated; one proposal places it in today's Shen county, Henan province, and others in Shandong." },
        { note: "A lost book: Sun Bin's military treatise disappeared for almost 2,000 years. A copy on bamboo strips was found in 1972 in a tomb at Yinqueshan, Shandong, and confirmed that he and Sun Tzu were two different people." },
        { note: "The end of Pang Juan: Sima Qian says he killed himself; other versions say he died in the arrows of the first volley." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  queroneia: {
    war: "Macedonian expansion into Greece, Fourth Sacred War (339–338 BC)",
    hook: "The day the Greek cities lost their freedom and an 18-year-old prince appeared to the world.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 359 BC, Macedonia was a poor kingdom in the north, invaded by its neighbors. Twenty years later, King Philip II had turned the country into the greatest military power in Greece, with a professional army and the [[phalanx|falange]] armed with the [[sarissa]].",
        "Philip advanced little by little, mixing war, bribery and diplomacy. In Athens, the orator Demosthenes spent years warning, in his speeches against Philip, that Greek freedom was at stake.",
        { h: "The road to Chaeronea" },
        { timeline: [
          ["346 BC", "Peace of Philocrates between Athens and Philip, who ends the Third Sacred War as arbiter of central Greece."],
          ["340 BC", "Philip besieges Byzantium, an ally of Athens, and the war resumes."],
          ["339 BC", "A religious dispute over sacred lands of Delphi gives Philip the pretext to come down into Greece."],
          ["End of 339 BC", "Philip occupies Elatea, a few days from Athens. Demosthenes persuades Athens and Thebes to ally."],
          ["August 338 BC", "The two armies meet on the plain of Chaeronea, in Boeotia."]
        ]},
        "The news of the taking of Elatea reached Athens at night and, according to Demosthenes, filled the city with panic. He went to Thebes and achieved the impossible: an alliance between the two old enemies. For months there were skirmishes in the mountain passes, until Philip got around the defenses and the allies fell back to Chaeronea."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Philip II of Macedonia, about 44 years old" },
        "He had lived as a hostage in Thebes in his youth, in the years of Epaminondas, and learned there much of what he knew about war. He had lost an eye at a siege. He was patient and preferred to win without fighting, but at Chaeronea he had the best army in Greece and wanted to settle the matter once and for all.",
        { h: "Alexander, 18 years old" },
        "Philip's son was given command of the left wing, facing the Thebans, with experienced generals at his side. It was his first great battle.",
        { h: "The allies" },
        "The Athenians were commanded by Chares and Lysicles, the Thebans by Theagenes. None of them had the talent of the Macedonian generals, and command was divided among cities that until recently had hated one another. Demosthenes fought as a simple [[hoplite|hoplita]]."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Diodorus gives Philip 30,000 foot soldiers and 2,000 horsemen, a number modern historians accept. For the allies there is no ancient number; moderns think they were roughly the same, some 30,000 to 35,000, almost all Athenians and Thebans, with contingents from Corinth, Megara, Achaea and other cities.",
        { note: "The only continuous narrative of the battle is Diodorus', short and vague. The rest comes from anecdotes in Polyaenus and Frontinus. So the reconstruction of the movements is probable, not certain." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The allies chose well. Their line, some 4 km long, ran from the slopes near the town of Chaeronea, on the left, to the Cephissus river, on the right. Both sides were protected. The Athenians stood on the left, the Thebans on the right, by the river, with the [[Sacred Band|batalhão sagrado]] at the end, and the other allies in the center.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Philip put himself on the right wing, facing the Athenians, with the [[hypaspists|hipaspistas]]. Alexander stood on the left, facing the Thebans, with the cavalry of the [[Companions|companheiros]]. The phalanx stood in the center.",
        "The fight was long and hard, says Diodorus. According to Polyaenus, Philip then withdrew with the right wing, slowly, in order. The Athenians, inexperienced, thought the Macedonians were fleeing. An Athenian general is said to have shouted to chase the Macedonians all the way to Macedonia. They advanced, left their position and came down onto open ground.",
        "To keep up with the Athenians, the allied center was pulled to the left, and the line stretched. Many historians reconstruct the decisive moment here: a gap opened between the allies and the Thebans, who had stayed put by the river.",
        "Through the gap came Alexander. Diodorus says he was the first to break the enemy line, and Plutarch, that he was the first to attack the Sacred Band. The Thebans were enveloped.",
        "At the other end, Philip stopped retreating and turned. The Athenians, disorganized after the pursuit and now on bad ground, were thrown back and fled. Demosthenes fled with them.",
        "The Sacred Band did not flee. Surrounded, it fought to the end. According to Plutarch, all 300 died where they stood, and Philip, on seeing the bodies piled up, wept and cursed anyone who thought ill of those men."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Diodorus counts more than 1,000 Athenians dead and 2,000 captured, with similar Theban losses. Macedonian losses are not known.",
        "Philip treated the two enemies in opposite ways. Thebes received a Macedonian garrison and even had to pay to collect its own dead. Athens got back its prisoners without ransom and the ashes of its dead, carried, according to tradition, by Alexander himself. Philip needed the Athenian fleet for his next plan: Persia.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In 337 BC, Philip gathered the Greek cities in the [[League of Corinth|liga de corinto]], with himself as commander of a war against Persia. Sparta was the only one to stay out. The era of the independent Greek city-state, which had given the world Athenian democracy, ended there. Philip was assassinated in 336 BC, before setting out, and the project passed to Alexander.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Lion of Chaeronea: a great marble lion still stands on the battlefield. Digging beneath it, in the 19th century, archaeologists found the remains of 254 men, in seven rows. Tradition links the tomb to the Sacred Band." },
        { note: "Cavalry or infantry? The idea that Alexander commanded the Companions and entered on horseback through the gap is the best known, but the sources do not say which troops he commanded. Some historians think he led a part of the phalanx." },
        { note: "Demosthenes fled the battle, and his enemies never let him forget it. Even so, he was the one chosen to give the funeral speech for the dead." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  isso: {
    war: "Alexander's conquest of the Persian Empire (334–330 BC)",
    hook: "Two armies that were looking for each other, passed without seeing each other and ended up fighting on a strip of land between the sea and the mountain.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the spring of 334 BC, Alexander crossed into Asia with some 40,000 men and defeated the Persian governors at the Granicus river. For a year and a half, he took the cities of the coast of Asia Minor, today's Turkey. At Gordium, according to tradition, he cut with his sword the knot that nobody could untie.",
        "The Persian king, Darius III, had until then left the war to his [[satraps|sátrapa]]. Now he came in person, with the imperial army assembled in Babylon.",
        { h: "The road to Issus" },
        { timeline: [
          ["May 334 BC", "Battle of the Granicus: Alexander defeats the satraps of Asia Minor."],
          ["334–333 BC", "Alexander takes the coastal cities and spends the winter inland."],
          ["Summer 333 BC", "At Tarsus, Alexander falls gravely ill after a bath in the river, and the army halts for weeks."],
          ["Autumn 333 BC", "Darius gathers the army in Syria, on the plain of Sochi."],
          ["November 333 BC", "The two armies pass each other without seeing each other and fight by the Pinarus river."]
        ]},
        "Darius waited on a wide plain in Syria, ideal for his cavalry. Alexander, who had passed the gulf and gone on south, to Myriandrus, was delayed. Darius grew tired of waiting and went after him, north of the Amanus mountains. The two armies passed one another, separated by the mountain.",
        "Darius came out on the other side, at Issus, behind Alexander, and found the sick and wounded the Macedonians had left there. He had their hands cut off. He had now cut Alexander's supply lines. But he had also traded the wide plain for a narrow strip between the sea and the mountains."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Alexander III of Macedonia, 23 years old" },
        "When he learned that Darius was behind him, Alexander could hardly believe it and sent a boat to check. Once confirmed, he turned the army around and marched back north at night. For him, the news was excellent: in the narrow space, the size of the Persian army weighed less.",
        { h: "Darius III" },
        "King for three years, with a reputation for bravery. He had a much larger army and a core of Greek [[mercenaries|mercenários]], the best [[hoplites|hoplita]] available outside Macedonia. His mistake was leaving the plain. Once in place, he chose a defensive position behind a river, which was sensible.",
        { h: "Parmenion" },
        "The veteran general commanded the left wing, by the sea, with the order not to move away from the beach, so as not to be [[outflanked|flanquear]]."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Alexander had some 40,000 men: about 24,000 heavy infantry, 13,000 light infantry and almost 6,000 horsemen. For Darius, the ancient sources give from 250,000 to 600,000 men, impossible numbers. Modern historians generally settle on up to some 100,000, and some far fewer.",
        { note: "Callisthenes, the historian who accompanied Alexander, measured the field. The Greek historian Polybius, two centuries later, did the sums and showed that the Persian army the sources describe simply would not fit there." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A coastal plain only a few kilometers wide, between the Gulf of Issus and the slopes of the Amanus, cut by the Pinarus river, with steep banks in some stretches. Which of the present-day rivers is the Pinarus is still debated.",
        { h: "The deployment" },
        "Darius put the heavy cavalry on his right, by the sea. In the center, behind the river, stood the Greek mercenaries and, beside them, the [[Cardaces|cárdaces]]. He himself stood in the middle, in a war chariot, with the royal guard. On the left, troops climbed the hills and bent forward, threatening the Macedonian flank.",
        "Alexander put Parmenion, with the Thessalian horsemen, on the left by the sea, and the [[phalanx|falange]] in the center. He himself stood on the right, with the [[hypaspists|hipaspistas]] and the [[Companions|companheiros]]. To deal with the troops on the hills, he sent the Agrianians and the archers, who pushed them upward.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "The battle" },
        "First, the Persian cavalry crossed the river by the sea and fell on Parmenion. The Thessalians fell back and returned to the charge, holding as best they could against a much larger force.",
        "Second, Alexander attacked on the right, crossing the river at a run to spend little time under the arrows. The Persian left gave way quickly.",
        "Third, the center suffered. The phalanx advanced together but, climbing the steep bank of the river, lost its alignment and opened gaps. Darius' Greek mercenaries entered through them. According to Arrian, some 120 prominent Macedonians died there, among them the officer Ptolemy, son of Seleucus.",
        "Fourth, Alexander, already on the other side, turned left, toward the Persian center, straight for Darius' chariot. The fighting around the king was violent, and Alexander was wounded in the thigh.",
        "Fifth, Darius fled. According to the accounts, first in the chariot and, when the ground got difficult, on horseback, abandoning his shield and royal cloak. The king's flight brought down the rest: the mercenaries, attacked on the flank, withdrew in order, and the Persian cavalry by the sea, on learning of the flight, also left. The pursuit lasted until nightfall."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Curtius speaks of about 150 Macedonians dead and 4,500 wounded. Estimates of Persian losses are between 20,000 and 40,000, most of them in the flight across the narrow terrain.",
        "In the Persian camp remained Darius' mother, Sisygambis, his wife, his daughters and the campaign treasury. Alexander treated the women with respect, which the ancient authors praised greatly. Years later, he married one of the daughters.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Darius offered Alexander a settlement, and then a larger one: territories, money and a daughter. Alexander refused. Instead of pursuing Darius, he went down the coast and took the bases of the Persian fleet, Tyre and Egypt. When the two met again, at Gaugamela, two years later, it was to decide the whole empire.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Alexander Mosaic: the most famous image from Antiquity of a battle, found at Pompeii, shows Alexander bareheaded charging Darius, who turns in his chariot. Most scholars think it represents Issus, though some say Gaugamela." },
        { note: "Darius' numbers: Callisthenes spoke of 30,000 horsemen and 30,000 mercenaries, which is already a lot. The 600,000 of Arrian and Plutarch are legendary." },
        { note: "Alexandria: Alexander founded a city near the battlefield, Alexandretta, today İskenderun, in Turkey." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  hidaspes: {
    war: "Alexander's campaign in India (327–325 BC)",
    hook: "A river swollen by the monsoon, a secret crossing on a stormy night and the toughest opponent Alexander ever faced.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 330 BC, with Darius dead, Alexander was master of the Persian Empire. But he did not stop. He spent three years fighting in the mountains of today's Afghanistan and Uzbekistan and, in 327 BC, came down into India, which for the Greeks was the end of the known world.",
        "The Punjab, in today's Pakistan, was divided among rival kingdoms. The king of Taxila, Ambhi, called Taxiles by the Greeks, received Alexander as an ally, because he had an enemy across the river Hydaspes: King Porus.",
        { h: "The road to the Hydaspes" },
        { timeline: [
          ["330 BC", "Darius III is assassinated. Alexander takes the title King of Asia."],
          ["329–327 BC", "Hard war in Bactria and Sogdiana."],
          ["327 BC", "Alexander crosses the Hindu Kush and descends into India."],
          ["Spring 326 BC", "Taxiles allies with Alexander. Porus refuses to submit."],
          ["May 326 BC", "The armies face each other on the banks of the Hydaspes, today the Jhelum river."]
        ]},
        "Alexander ordered Porus to meet him and pay tribute. According to tradition, Porus replied that he would indeed come, but armed and at the head of his army."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Alexander III of Macedonia, 29 years old" },
        "He had already won at the Granicus, Issus and Gaugamela. At the Hydaspes, the problem was not the size of the enemy but the river. One could not cross a wide, fast river head on with [[war elephants|elefantes de guerra]] waiting on the other side: the horses panicked at their smell and noise.",
        { h: "Porus" },
        "King of the Pauravas, between the Hydaspes and Acesines rivers. The Greeks say he was huge, more than two meters tall, and that he fought mounted on an elephant. The historian Peter Green considered him the most capable opponent Alexander faced. His plan was simple and good: stay on the bank and prevent the crossing.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Craterus, one of Alexander's most trusted generals, stayed in the main camp with a good part of the army. Coenus commanded the cavalry maneuver that decided the battle."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Alexander's army in India had some 40,000 to 47,000 men, but only a part crossed the river: according to Arrian, some 5,000 horsemen and 6,000 foot soldiers, a number many historians think is low.",
        "Arrian gives Porus 30,000 foot soldiers, 4,000 horsemen, 300 chariots and 200 elephants. Other sources vary, above all on the number of elephants, which runs from 85 to 200. Modern estimates for Porus' army are between 20,000 and 50,000.",
        { h: "The game of cat and mouse" },
        "It was May, and the rains and the melting Himalayan snow had swollen the river. Alexander set up camp on the north bank, right in front of Porus, and began to pretend. He had wheat brought from all over the region, like someone preparing to wait for the dry season. At night, he made the cavalry run along the bank, shouting and blowing trumpets, as if about to cross. Porus sent the elephants to shadow them. After several nights of this, Porus stopped reacting.",
        "Meanwhile, Alexander found the right place: some 27 km upstream, where there was a bend with a wooded hill and a tree-covered island in the middle of the current, hiding the bank.",
        { h: "The crossing" },
        "Alexander left Craterus in the camp, with orders to cross only if Porus came out with the elephants. According to some accounts, he also left a double wearing the royal armor in plain sight. He set out at night with the chosen force and took dismantled boats and leather floats stuffed with straw.",
        "It rained heavily that night, with thunder that drowned out the noise. At dawn they crossed. On arrival, they discovered they had landed on another island, not on the bank. They had to look for a ford and cross again, with water up to their chests.",
        "Porus sent a son with cavalry and some 120 chariots, according to Arrian, to prevent the landing. It was too late. The chariots bogged down in the mud, and Alexander destroyed them. Porus' son died there.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Porus left a few elephants watching Craterus and went with the bulk of the army to face Alexander on firm, sandy ground. He put the elephants in front, spaced apart, and the infantry behind them, like a wall with towers. On the wings, cavalry and chariots.",
        "Alexander did not attack the elephants. First, he sent the Dahae horse archers against the Indian left wing. Then he charged with the [[Companions|companheiros]] against the cavalry of that wing.",
        "At the same time, Coenus, with two squadrons, went around the Indian line on the other side. When the Indian cavalry of the right came to help the left, Coenus fell on its back. Squeezed from both sides, the Indian cavalry took refuge among the elephants.",
        "Then the elephants advanced against the [[phalanx|falange]]. It was the most terrible part of the day. The Macedonians opened space, threw javelins at the mahouts and struck the animals' legs with axes and curved swords. The elephants, wounded and with no driver, went mad and began trampling Macedonians and Indians alike.",
        "Trapped, the Indian army was crushed between the phalanx, which advanced with locked shields, and the cavalry, which closed the ring from behind: a [[hammer and anvil|martelo e bigorna]]. Craterus crossed the river and fell on those fleeing.",
        "Porus, wounded, kept fighting to the end, unlike Darius. Alexander sent several emissaries. Taxiles himself went, and Porus nearly killed him with a spear. Only a friend of his, Meroes, managed to persuade him to stop."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Arrian says the Indians lost about 23,000 men; Diodorus speaks of 12,000 dead and more than 9,000 prisoners. Two sons of Porus died. For the Macedonians, Arrian counts only some 310 dead, and Diodorus about 1,000, a number that historians such as J. F. C. Fuller think more realistic.",
        "When Porus arrived, Alexander asked how he wished to be treated. 'Like a king,' Porus answered, according to Arrian. Alexander liked the answer, gave him back his kingdom and even enlarged it. Porus was loyal to the end.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was Alexander's last great pitched battle and perhaps the hardest. He went on east to the Hyphasis river, but there his soldiers, after eight years of marching and with news of even larger armies ahead, refused to continue. Alexander stayed three days shut in his tent and gave in. He turned back south, down the Indus, and died in Babylon in 323 BC.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Bucephalus: Alexander's horse died during or just after the battle, of wounds or of old age; he was about 30 years old. Alexander founded two cities there: Nicaea ('victory') and Bucephala, in honor of the horse." },
        { note: "Victory or draw? Some modern Indian authors question the Greek version and suggest that the battle was less decisive. There are no Indian sources from the time about it, only Greek and Roman ones, written centuries later." },
        { note: "Alexander's double left in the camp appears in one ancient account, but historians treat it as possible, not certain." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

